#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0670172-77cb-5e88-b78d-e5136c06095f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1596b302-488d-52ec-b893-25831ccfd734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321127} {:answer/id #uuid "12f9e085-229a-5477-a113-3b693a34550f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\frac{4}{13},\\frac{6}{13}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321128} {:answer/id #uuid "17699248-2fe7-5e8b-932f-b2cbf1cfc70a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\frac{2}{73},\\frac{3}{73}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321129} {:answer/id #uuid "60b69ecc-1940-5d08-8109-42cd6b7098e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\frac{4}{73},\\frac{6}{73}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321130} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0c760e-2668-5284-a218-14ba16d6f385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\frac{2}{13},\\frac{3}{13}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12f9e085-229a-5477-a113-3b693a34550f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\frac{4}{13},\\frac{6}{13}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321128} :answer-field/id #uuid "c67e4227-9229-5149-b649-60f2d95ff7ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2862ba25-b3dd-52fe-babb-88481e3608c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-101541" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{13}⟨0,2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{4}{13},\\frac{6}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af810eeb-2e02-5de1-9a92-78e849928924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321134} {:answer/id #uuid "9852b6af-fd76-5843-a953-8840419120f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321135} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f892ab-2f7f-59da-84a9-e11e66f71bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321136} {:answer/id #uuid "2a59e46b-f97a-5d54-a12f-48e06ab3c12d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321137} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c23517-1d73-5b59-bccd-79e259cffab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af810eeb-2e02-5de1-9a92-78e849928924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321134} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fcc8a73-a7fe-596a-930a-66c45e5d8b45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c567d1-1fe1-5af5-9c79-4a83502f3a73" :question/math-academy-id "q-116604" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}-9x<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =x({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x(x+3)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9743fee-4ceb-53a8-bcb9-9997b69753cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084592} {:answer/id #uuid "d72d2d76-d019-5daa-bd50-e10b7b7e18cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084593} {:answer/id #uuid "239fa676-259c-5de4-8aed-86e6435e4eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084594} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc8fe1e-8841-508d-97a7-f718dc4ab5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084595} {:answer/id #uuid "69e358df-3f7d-5c4f-b712-83b927d19387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72d2d76-d019-5daa-bd50-e10b7b7e18cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084593} :answer-field/id #uuid "d95f96f1-a60b-5b18-af4d-e457b7e566e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee61babb-f6b1-5a98-9886-55df60a724bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-11723" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}k & k \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =k\\cdot 1-k\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =k-2k \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k & =0 \\\\ k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $k$ is $k=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7cb0146-57a8-525b-9db7-7cb84de3e3d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}(y + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093981} {:answer/id #uuid "ef628fc7-7677-539a-a421-c8de69a34297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} =-\\frac{8}{3}(y + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093982} {:answer/id #uuid "32574fd9-f816-572c-86fc-b3f8b724446a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} = \\frac{8}{3}(y + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093983} {:answer/id #uuid "37d2b3d6-7be3-5eb7-ac5e-2c77df387ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 4)^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}(y - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093984} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4fb806-9d2d-5c69-bf6d-e8124a15cae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} =-\\frac{4}{3}(y + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7cb0146-57a8-525b-9db7-7cb84de3e3d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}(y + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093981} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4da476b-d326-5425-ae19-b6d340aeba24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "222815fb-4b3f-5e30-b0ad-7e0bc5298f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11823" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043368/assets/267d5f0aabe29da84842d2edd19276521640b838cfb1777e6932e7472716c04f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(2,-4),$ and passes through the point $(6,8).$\n\nWe know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(6,8),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 16 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})(y+4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8041938c-b394-5c87-9c43-eb0775333a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321141} {:answer/id #uuid "b2975091-274c-575a-9ca4-99f242ca3d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321142} {:answer/id #uuid "e23dcaae-343e-5c6f-ab58-e9e96baff3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2818cb-bca7-5dbf-ab48-fe00791e051c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321144} {:answer/id #uuid "28774533-52cb-5376-a3bd-b7e727f3538f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e23dcaae-343e-5c6f-ab58-e9e96baff3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca9088a-3574-5fb0-a2c2-bca04f47d657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4bd1c51-7cdb-5faf-a2e7-d35b9e8b6eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143077" :question/problem "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a food container decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the container $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a food container **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the temperature.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature $T$* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the temperature $T.$* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "667ac53c-36b8-56a0-9699-fe27c8373c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum or Difference of Three or More Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c650158-f318-5e50-96ea-e45fc2fc3ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c650158-f318-5e50-96ea-e45fc2fc3ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321148} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc374a5f-3d42-5ac1-8de6-f83ccff898e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f02b02d6-6952-5118-a270-006e92b1aeba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f02b02d6-6952-5118-a270-006e92b1aeba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321150} :answer-field/id #uuid "62258083-b050-55d4-bb65-de3d2406fd17" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a5a0eb3-4ebb-5615-a055-371ba5534788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a5a0eb3-4ebb-5615-a055-371ba5534788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321152} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b9bdd5b-0ae3-5fca-9e0b-47da07018637" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321151} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9604be09-564d-5389-9bb0-58a2200407ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9604be09-564d-5389-9bb0-58a2200407ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321154} :answer-field/id #uuid "d750a881-2647-5d6e-8e3b-3674d6b95ad5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bd04c9e-65fe-587f-81f5-e3a3e40c5df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-279528" :question/problem "Calculate $X+(Y-Z)$, where\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $X+(Y-Z)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Performing the operations on each corresponding entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+(Y-Z) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+([\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}9-2 & 8-3 \\\\ 3-1 & 7-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+7 & 6+5 \\\\ 2+2 & 5+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 11 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76b0ad1e-827e-5795-8431-3276c8eed881" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e588f20-ef86-59dc-b62d-12450320dfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no minimum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321157} {:answer/id #uuid "d0130e26-5716-5576-bccb-4a079be6c23e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321158} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb74260-e1ac-53b3-aaf8-07c5aaab753a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321159} {:answer/id #uuid "7af40bf4-e02b-5faa-a7f1-7ad58226c461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321160} {:answer/id #uuid "63f214f1-3138-58b7-ab40-ee1317c611bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f214f1-3138-58b7-ab40-ee1317c611bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321161} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a419ac6-8070-557e-89c9-36a16c0427e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "794a2260-974a-5a5a-af52-bb91dcf1ebd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42424" :question/problem "The minimum value of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043368/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the minimum value of the function is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef6ef49e-98bb-5466-b00a-d02e9e8f0544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321164} {:answer/id #uuid "88231c56-e258-5fd9-b5c4-f579da9eefe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321165} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5c3c9e-99b7-5caa-8971-f3c3bb3601d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321166} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7a48c5-07e9-5e4c-8dee-377c497be27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321167} {:answer/id #uuid "54643908-4cd0-5504-adfc-d57785d735a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88231c56-e258-5fd9-b5c4-f579da9eefe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321165} :answer-field/id #uuid "26cbde72-b856-544f-a548-9b54f3b0231b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33594db7-6d24-536b-a84c-855f2f373c65" :question/math-academy-id "q-7338" :question/problem "Express $8{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-4$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n4(2{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-1)\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={15}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {15}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-1) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ba3c1ac-1ca3-53f9-9308-5dbed69fe9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321171} {:answer/id #uuid "38d4d7e0-5da4-5645-b006-8c7f1c360922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321172} {:answer/id #uuid "24656f8e-6f94-520f-ab9c-fbbe4d0c767e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321173} {:answer/id #uuid "23cd7783-6f26-59bd-8a76-c51062d1355e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321174} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e97a09-456b-51cc-aa37-6aaf29b3b263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ba3c1ac-1ca3-53f9-9308-5dbed69fe9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321171} :answer-field/id #uuid "0806ce5c-aacd-57fa-ad36-4fa5e71bdd78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "993a5199-6dff-5393-ad8f-667e046f8498" :question/math-academy-id "q-89052" :question/problem "A pet center offers veterinary services to dogs and cats. Of the total patients, $\\frac{3}{5}$\nare dogs and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are cats. Also, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the dogs are vaccinated while $\\frac{7}{15}$ of all pets are vaccinated. What is the probability that a randomly selected cat assisted by the center is vaccinated?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the event that the chosen pet is vaccinated, and let $D$ be the event that the pet is a dog. Then ${D}^{′}$ is the event that the pet is a cat.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pet is vaccinated, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(V)=P(V|D)P(D)+P(V|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the pets in the center are dogs and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are cats, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P({D}^{′})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the dogs are vaccinated, we have\n\n$$\nP(V|D)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{7}{15}$ of the pets are vaccinated, we have\n\n$$\nP(V)=\\frac{7}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(V|{D}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V|D)P(D)+P(V|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{15} & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{3}{5})+P(V|{D}^{′})(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{15} & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}P(V|{D}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}P(V|{D}^{′}) & =\\frac{7-3}{15} \\\\ P(V|{D}^{′}) & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cat assisted in the center is vaccinated is $\\frac{2}{3}.$"}]}
